## Decoding the question
"**Compare**" means set the three cases side by side on **common themes** — not three separate summaries. Keep each case summary short, then organise most of the answer by the **four themes** the question names: **violence, voice, the state and the media**. A comparison of the three case studies has **never been asked** — a blind spot — but each case has been asked singly (Mukhtaran Mai ==2019==, Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy ==2020==, Malala ==2025==).

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) The three cases
**Mukhtaran Mai**, a survivor of *panchayat*-ordered gang rape; **Malala Yousafzai**, a schoolgirl shot by the Taliban for demanding education; and **Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy**, a filmmaker who documented acid attacks and honour killing.

#### ii) The argument
Together they show that violence against women in Pakistan is rooted in **patriarchal power** — tribal, militant and familial — and sustained by **impunity**. Each woman turned violence into **voice** by a different route: survival, activism and documentation. The **state** was absent or slow until the **media** — national and global — forced a response. Their courage changed laws and debates; it could not by itself change the structures that produce violence.

### 2. Mukhtaran Mai

#### i) The crime
On ==22 June 2002==, in **Meerwala**, Muzaffargarh (South Punjab), a *panchayat* dominated by the powerful **Mastoi** clan ordered the gang rape of Mukhtaran Mai, of the weaker **Tatla** clan, to "punish" an accusation against her twelve-year-old brother.

#### ii) The legal journey
An anti-terrorism court sentenced six men to death (==2002==); the Lahore High Court acquitted five (==2005==); the **Supreme Court** upheld the acquittals on ==21 April 2011==.

#### iii) Her response
She refused silence or suicide, used her compensation to open her village's first **girls' school**, founded a women's welfare organisation and wrote ***In the Name of Honor*** (==2006==).

### 3. Malala Yousafzai

#### i) The context
In **Swat**, the Taliban under **Maulana Fazlullah** banned girls' education from ==2009== and destroyed more than a hundred girls' schools.

#### ii) The attack
Malala, who had written a **BBC Urdu** diary as "**Gul Makai**" (==2009==), was shot in the head on her school bus on ==9 October 2012==.

#### iii) Her advocacy
She addressed the **United Nations** on ==12 July 2013==, founded the **Malala Fund** (==2013==) and shared the **Nobel Peace Prize** in ==2014== with Kailash Satyarthi, at **17** the youngest laureate.

### 4. Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy

#### i) The filmmaker
Born in Karachi in ==1978==, she became Pakistan's first Oscar winner — and won twice.

#### ii) The two films

##### (a) Saving Face (Oscar 2012)
Co-directed with **Daniel Junge**, it followed acid-attack survivors **Zakia** and **Rukhsana** and surgeon **Dr Mohammad Jawad**, alongside the passage of the **Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act 2011**.

##### (b) A Girl in the River (Oscar 2016)
It told the story of **Saba Qaiser**, shot by her father and uncle near Gujranwala for marrying by choice, who survived but was pressured to **forgive** them.

### 5. Violence: three forms, one root

#### i) Three perpetrators
A tribal ***panchayat*** (Mukhtaran Mai), a **militant** movement (Malala), and **husbands and fathers** (Sharmeen's subjects).

#### ii) One root
In every case violence punished a woman or girl for transgressing a male-defined order — of **clan honour**, of **religious control**, of **family *izzat***.

#### iii) In Galtung's terms
**Direct** violence (rape, shooting, acid) rested on **structural** violence (parallel justice, forgiveness laws, weak policing) and **cultural** violence (honour, misused religion).

::: fig grid | The three case studies compared.
@cols 3
Mukhtaran Mai: survivor of panchayat-ordered gang rape (2002); schools; In the Name of Honor (2006)
Malala Yousafzai: shot by the Taliban (2012); UN speech (2013); Nobel Peace Prize (2014)
Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy: filmmaker; Saving Face (2012) on acid; A Girl in the River (2016) on honour
Violence: clan "justice"
Violence: militant control of education
Violence: family honour and revenge
