State: laws of 2011 and 2016 after the films
:::

### 6. Voice: survivor, activist, witness

#### i) The survivor
Mukhtaran Mai broke the expected silence and spoke as a **survivor**, turning personal violence into public testimony.

#### ii) The activist
Malala spoke **before** and after the attack, as an **activist** for a right — education — rather than only as a victim.

#### iii) The witness
Sharmeen spoke as a **witness**, giving other women's stories a national and global audience — and showing survivors' agency, as when Zakia sought justice.

### 7. The state: absent, slow, then responsive

#### i) Absent
The state failed to prevent the crimes: *panchayat* justice in Meerwala; the Taliban's control of Swat until the ==2009== military operation; impunity for acid attackers and honour killers.

#### ii) Slow and defensive
Mukhtaran Mai's case ended in **acquittals**; the government placed her on the **Exit Control List** in ==2005== (later reversed), and President Musharraf's remark about women "crying rape" provoked outrage.

#### iii) Responsive under pressure
After the films and public outrage, the state legislated: the **Acid Act 2011**, the **honour-killing law of October 2016** — after Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif said "there is no honour in honour killing" — and constitutional commitment to education under **Article 25A**.

### 8. The media: amplifier and battleground

#### i) Amplifier
A local imam's sermon and a journalist made Mukhtaran Mai's case national and global; the BBC diary and international coverage made Malala known; Sharmeen's films reached Oscar audiences and Pakistani television.

#### ii) Battleground
The same media spread **conspiracy theories** — Malala as a "Western agent", an "**I am not Malala**" day by a private schools' federation in ==2014== — and the charge that the films "defamed Pakistan".

### 9. The "image of Pakistan" charge

#### i) The charge
Critics say these cases "wash Pakistan's dirty linen in public" and serve foreign agendas.

#### ii) The reply
Silence protects abusers. The cases also show **Pakistani reformers** — lawyers, doctors, legislators, the women themselves — and they led to Pakistani laws. Shame belongs to the violence, not to those who expose it.

### 10. What they achieved

#### i) Mukhtaran Mai
Schools in Meerwala, a women's organisation, and a voice in the debate that preceded the **Protection of Women Act 2006**.

#### ii) Malala
Global attention to girls' education, the **Malala Fund**'s grants in Pakistan and beyond, and a symbol of resistance to militancy.

#### iii) Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy
Public debate that helped secure the **Acid Act 2011** and the **honour-killing law 2016**.

### 11. What they could not change

#### i) Impunity
Mukhtaran Mai's attackers were acquitted; honour killers still win pardons — **Qandeel Baloch**'s brother was acquitted in ==2022==.

#### ii) Structures
More than **25 million** children remain out of school, most of them girls; *panchayats* and *jirgas* still operate.

#### iii) The limits of individual courage
Individual courage and media attention can **start** change; only state action and social change can **complete** it.

### 12. Lessons for Pakistan

#### i) End parallel justice
Prosecute *jirgas* and *panchayats* that order violence.

#### ii) End impunity
Make crimes against women **non-compoundable**, strengthen investigation and protect witnesses.

#### iii) Use the media responsibly
Support survivor-centred journalism and documentary, and counter disinformation.

#### iv) Educate girls
Enforce **Article 25A**, so that Malala's cause becomes national policy.

### 13. Conclusion

#### i) The comparison in brief
Three women, three forms of violence — clan, militant and familial — and three routes to voice: survivor, activist and witness.

#### ii) The verdict
Their stories reveal a state that was absent or slow until the media forced it to act, and a society divided between those who saw them as heroines and those who saw them as threats to Pakistan's image. Their legacy is real — schools, laws, global attention — but incomplete until Pakistan ends the **impunity** their cases exposed.

## Why this answer scores
- **Compares** on the four themes the question names, rather than summarising three cases separately.
- Keeps each case summary short but **dated and precise** (22 June 2002; 21 April 2011; 9 October 2012; Oscars 2012 and 2016).
- Uses **Galtung** to show the common root of the three forms of violence.
- Addresses the **"image of Pakistan"** charge fairly.
- A **grid** figure sets the three cases side by side.
